<p>In 1782, two years after his death, a small London book appeared in two volumes with the title <em>The Letters of the Late Ignatius Sancho, an African</em>. It opened with a short life written by a young lawyer named Joseph Jekyll, and Jekyll began where the story actually began: on a ship, in the Middle Passage, some days out from the coast of Guinea, where a child was born in 1729 and baptized at Cartagena by a bishop who gave him the name Ignatius.</p>

<p>Then Jekyll explained why the child arrived in England at the age of two and could not read. His owner gave him to three unmarried sisters living at Greenwich. And the sisters had a theory of education, which Jekyll set down without softening it:</p>

<blockquote>Their prejudices had unhappily taught them, that African ignorance was the only security for his obedience, and that to enlarge the mind of their slave would go near to emancipate his person.</blockquote>

<p>That is the whole argument of this story in a single sentence, written by a man who was not a radical. Illiteracy was not neglect. It was policy. A boy who could read was a boy who could leave.</p>

