<h2>Eighteen Years in a House With Books and No Permission</h2>

<p>The sisters who raised him were not monsters in the ordinary sense. They were respectable Greenwich women who took in a small child, gave him a bed and a name, and taught him to serve. The name was a joke at his expense: they called him Sancho, Jekyll says, "from a fancied resemblance to the Squire of Don Quixote". He was a servant in a house where he was also the punchline.</p>

<p>What he did have, from about the age of seven, was a visitor. The Duke of Montagu kept a house at Blackheath, a short walk from Greenwich, and he saw the boy and liked him. Jekyll's account is precise about what the Duke did and what he could not do:</p>

<blockquote>He brought him frequently home to the Duchess, indulged his turn for reading with presents of books, and strongly recommended to his mistresses the duty of cultivating a genius of such apparent fertility. His mistresses, however, were inflexible, and even threatened on angry occasions to return Ignatius Sancho to his African slavery.</blockquote>

<p>Read that threat properly. The sisters were not withholding lessons. They were answering an argument about literacy with a reminder that he was property, and that the property could be sent back across the Atlantic. A child of ten cannot be told a thing more effective than that.</p>

<p>So the reading happened anyway, in whatever cracks were available - in borrowed books returned before they were missed, in the margins of a day built entirely out of other people's errands. Nobody recorded the method, which is the central frustration of this story. We know the result with great precision and the process hardly at all. What we can say is that by his early twenties he had read enough eighteenth-century English prose to write it, imitate it, and argue with it, without a single lesson that anybody thought worth writing down.</p>

<h2>Five Shillings and an Old Pistol</h2>

<p>In 1749 the Duke died, and the arrangement that had made the Greenwich house tolerable died with him. Sancho was twenty. He ran away to Montagu House at Blackheath and asked the Duchess for protection, and she sent him away with a reprimand.</p>

<p>What he did next is the part of the story that genteel biographies used to leave out, and it is the part that tells you how bad the alternative was. Jekyll again:</p>

<blockquote>Enamoured still of that liberty, the scope of whose enjoyment was now limited to his last five shillings, and resolute to maintain it with life, he procured an old pistol for purposes which his father's example had suggested as familiar, and had sanctified as hereditary.</blockquote>

<p>His father, Jekyll tells us, had "defeated the miseries of slavery by an act of suicide" soon after the family's arrival in New Granada. The son, at twenty, with five shillings and no legal personhood, prepared to do the same. The Duchess, reading the situation correctly, relented. She took him into her household as a butler, and he stayed until her death in 1751, when he found himself - by her bequest and his own saving - possessed of seventy pounds in money and an annuity of thirty pounds a year.</p>

<p>He was free. He was, by the standards of a Greenwich servant, solvent. And he had one asset that no bequest could have given him: about eighteen years of reading in a house that had tried to prevent it.</p>

<h2>The Voice, and the Closed Stage Door</h2>

<p>One detail in Jekyll's memoir is worth a paragraph of its own, because it is the sort of thing a modern learner will recognize instantly.</p>

<p>Sancho loved the theater to the point of obsession. He would spend his last shilling at Drury Lane to see David Garrick act. Then, Jekyll says, he was "induced to consider the stage as a resource in the hour of adversity; and his complexion suggested an offer to the manager of attempting Othello" - and the offer failed, not because of his color alone but because of "a defective and incorrigible articulation".</p>

<p>He had a speech impediment. The man who would become one of the great prose stylists of his age could not reliably perform his own sentences aloud. So the stage closed, and he did the only thing available to somebody with a difficult mouth and an excellent ear: he put everything on paper. The letters that made him famous are, among other things, a monument to a man who chose the medium his voice would allow.</p>

<p>What he read, he imitated. Jekyll describes the years after his marriage this way: "A commerce with the Muses was supported amid the trivial and momentary interruptions of a shop; the Poets were studied, and even imitated with some success." He wrote two plays. He composed. Sixty-two of his compositions survive, printed in four collections in London between roughly 1767 and 1779: <em>Minuets, Cotillons and Country Dances</em> book I, with 24 dances; <em>A Collection of New Songs</em>, six settings of Shakespeare, Garrick and Anacreon; a second dance book with 20 more; and <em>Twelve Country Dances for the Year 1779</em>. He wrote a <em>Theory of Music</em> and dedicated it to the Princess Royal, though no copy has survived. He published letters and articles in newspapers under his own name and under the pen name Africanus.</p>

<h2>The Letter That Made Him a Public Figure</h2>

<p>In 1766, at the height of the British debate over the slave trade, Sancho sat down and wrote to Laurence Sterne, the most fashionable novelist in the country, and asked him to use his platform:</p>

<blockquote>That subject, handled in your striking manner, would ease the yoke (perhaps) of many - but if only of one - Gracious God! - what a feast to a benevolent heart!</blockquote>

<p>Sterne answered on 27 July 1766, and the reply became a document of the abolitionist movement. He wrote of "the finest tints, and most insensible gradations" by which nature descends "from the fairest face about St. James's, to the sootiest complexion in Africa", and asked at which shade the ties of blood were supposed to stop. The exchange was reprinted, and Sancho - a shopkeeper who had been given away as a child - became a name in the newspapers.</p>

<p>He knew what that fame was worth and what it cost. Later, in the 1779 letter to William Stevenson, he signed off with the joke and the dignity in the same breath:</p>

<blockquote>I am Sir an Affrican - with two ffs - if you please - &amp; proud am I to be of a country that knows no politicians - nor lawyers - nor thieves of any denomination save Natural.</blockquote>

<p>And of the country he had lived in since he was two, he wrote something no admirer of the age liked to quote: that he was "only a lodger, and hardly that".</p>

<h2>Grocer, Voter, Correspondent</h2>

<p>By 1773 gout and weight had ended his career as a valet in the Montagu family. In 1774, with help from the family that had half-protected him for thirty years, he opened a grocery shop at 19 Charles Street in Westminster, selling tobacco, sugar and tea. He was selling goods that were themselves produced by enslaved people in the Caribbean, which is the moral knot at the center of his adult life and one he did not pretend to untie. He wrote about commerce as a potential blessing - "attended with strict honesty - and with Religion for its companion" - and about the "abominable traffic for slaves" with a clarity his customers can only have found uncomfortable.</p>

<p>The shop became a meeting place. David Garrick came. The violinist Felice Giardini came. Joseph Nollekens, the sculptor, gave him a plaster cast of his bust of Sterne. Charles James Fox, who would later steer the abolition resolution through Parliament, visited a grocer on Charles Street. And because he owned property, Sancho could vote. Voting was open in those days; the record shows him voting for Hugh Percy and Thomas Pelham-Clinton in 1774, and for George Brydges Rodney and Charles James Fox in 1780. He is the second person of African origin known to have voted in a British general election, after the victualler John London.</p>

<p>He died on 14 December 1780, of the gout that had already reshaped his life, and was buried at St Margaret's, Westminster. He was the first person of African descent known to have been given an obituary in the British press.</p>

<h2>What Happened to the Letters</h2>

<p>Frances Crewe, one of his correspondents, collected 160 of his letters and published them in 1782. More than two thousand people subscribed. His widow received over five hundred pounds in royalties, a sum worth roughly seventy-four thousand pounds today, and four further editions appeared by 1803. The editor's stated purpose was blunt and double-edged: "the desire of showing that an untutored African may possess abilities equal to an European; and the still superior motive, of wishing to serve his worthy family".</p>

<p>It is worth noticing what that framing gives and what it takes. It made the book publishable, and it made the author a case study. Two centuries of readers admired the letters as evidence and only later began reading them as literature. But the letters survive, and they survive as the first published collection of letters by a writer of African descent - a first that cannot be unmade.</p>

<p>His son William Leach Osborne Sancho took over the shop and turned it into a printing and bookselling business, and in 1803 printed the fifth edition of his father's letters, with a frontispiece engraved by Bartolozzi. The shop that sold sugar became a shop that sold books.</p>

<h2>What a Self-Taught Learner Can Take From Him</h2>

<p>Sancho never sat an exam, never enrolled anywhere, and left no method. But the record does show five things he did, and every one of them is available to a learner with a library card.</p>

<h3>1. He read above his station, on borrowed time</h3>

<p>The Duke's library was not a graded reader. It was an eighteenth-century gentleman's collection - Pope, Shakespeare, the essayists, the poets - and Sancho read it because it was there and because somebody kept putting it in his hands. The lesson is not that hard texts are always right. It is that access to a good shelf, even an interrupted one, beats a perfect curriculum you never open.</p>

<h3>2. He studied and imitated, in that order</h3>

<p>"The Poets were studied, and even imitated with some success." Study, then imitation, is the oldest writing method in the world and it is still the fastest. Copy a paragraph you admire by hand. Then write your own version of it. Sancho's prose is not naive; it is the work of a man who had absorbed a style so completely he could bend it.</p>

<h3>3. He wrote to real readers, not to exercises</h3>

<p>Every surviving Sancho letter had an addressee who would answer. He wrote to Sterne about abolition, to Garrick about the stage, to newspaper editors under a pen name, to friends about gout and grief and the price of tea. Output with an audience attached is a different activity from output in a workbook, and the letters show the difference on every page.</p>

<h3>4. He used the language for things he wanted</h3>

<p>Music, commerce, politics, friendship. The vocabulary of the counting house and the vocabulary of the concert hall both entered his prose because both were part of his life. This is the same principle that makes gaming or fandom work for modern learners: language attached to something you are actually trying to do gets retained, and language attached to nothing does not.</p>

<h3>5. He kept going after the stage door closed</h3>

<p>A speech impediment shut him out of the one public use of language he most wanted. He did not stop writing; he wrote harder, in the form his voice allowed. Every learner has some equivalent closed door - an accent they cannot shift, a memory that will not hold paradigms, a schedule that eats the study hour. Sancho's answer was not to fix the impediment. It was to find the medium where it did not matter.</p>

<h2>The Line Jekyll Wrote and Probably Did Not Intend</h2>

<p>Jekyll's memoir is the work of a man with the prejudices of his time, and it is full of the condescension that made the book sell. He apologizes for his subject's station: "Of a Negro, a Butler, and a Grocer, there are but slender anecdotes to animate the page of the biographer." He means it as an excuse for a thin biography.</p>

<p>And then, a few pages later, he writes the sentence that has outlived every assumption behind it:</p>

<blockquote>He who surveys the extent of intellect to which Ignatius Sancho had attained by self-education, will perhaps conclude, that the perfection of the reasoning faculties does not depend on a peculiar conformation of the skull or the colour of a common integument.</blockquote>

<p>A lawyer in 1782, writing a preface for a dead grocer, ended up arguing against the scientific racism that was about to become respectable for the next century and a half. He got there by looking at what one self-taught man had managed to write in a house that had tried to keep him from writing at all.</p>

<blockquote>The sisters were right about one thing, and it is the only thing they were right about: an education does emancipate a person. They simply could not stop it from happening.</blockquote>

<p>Two hundred and forty years later there is a plaque on the surviving wall of Montagu House in Greenwich, a Royal Mail stamp, a Google Doodle, a play by Paterson Joseph, and a café in Greenwich Park that carries his name. None of that was the point. The point is the two volumes from 1782, still in print, still readable, written by a man whose first library was lent to him by a duke and forbidden to him by the people he lived with.</p>

